      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after applying the two successive discounts, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the sale price of the shirt after the first discount of 20%.\n2. Calculate the price after applying the second discount of 25% on the sale price.\n3. Determine the single percent discount that would result in the same final price as the two successive discounts.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the sale price after the first discount.**\n\nThe original price of the shirt is \\$30. The first discount is 20% off. Therefore, the sale price is:\n\\[\n30 - 0.20 \\times 30 = 30 - 6 = 24\n\\]\nSo, the sale price is \\$24.\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the price after applying the second discount of 25% on the sale price.**\n\nThe second discount is 25% off the sale price of \\$24. Therefore, the price after the second discount is:\n\\[\n24 - 0.25 \\times 24 = 24 - 6 = 18\n\\]\nSo, the final price after both discounts is \\$18.\n\n**Step 3: Determine the single percent discount that would give the same final price.**\n\nLet \\( x \\) be the single percent discount that would result in a final price of \\$18 from the original price of \\$30. The price after a single discount of \\( x \\) percent off \\$30 is:\n\\[\n30 - \\frac{x}{100} \\times 30 = 30 \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) = 30 \\left(\\frac{100 - x}{100}\\right)\n\\]\nWe set this equal to the final price of \\$18:\n\\[\n30 \\left(\\frac{100 - x}{100}\\right) = 18\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{30(100 - x)}{100} = 18\n\\]\n\\[\n30(100 - x) = 18 \\times 100\n\\]\n\\[\n3000 - 30x = 1800\n\\]\n\\[\n3000 - 1800 = 30x\n\\]\n\\[\n1200 = 30x\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1200}{30} = 40\n\\]\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
