      "text": "To determine the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the sale price of the shirt after the first discount of 20%.\n2. Calculate the price after the additional 25% discount on the sale price.\n3. Determine the single discount that would result in the same final price as the combined effect of the two successive discounts.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the sale price after the first discount.**\n\nThe original price of the shirt is $30. A 20% discount means the shirt is sold for 80% of its original price.\n\\[\n\\text{Sale price} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the price after the additional 25% discount on the sale price.**\n\nThe price after the first discount is $24. A 25% discount on $24 means the shirt is sold for 75% of the sale price.\n\\[\n\\text{Final price} = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Determine the single discount that would result in the same final price.**\n\nThe final price after the two successive discounts is $18. The original price of the shirt was $30, so the discount on the original price is:\n\\[\n\\text{Original price} - \\text{Final price} = 30 - 18 = 12\n\\]\nThe single percentage discount is:\n\\[\n\\text{Single discount} = \\left( \\frac{12}{30} \\right) \\times 100\\% = 40\\%\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
