      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after successive discounts, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the price after the 20% off sale:**\n   The shirt is originally priced at $30. A 20% discount means the price is reduced by 20% of $30.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 0.20 \\times 30 = 6\n   \\]\n   So, the price after the 20% discount is:\n   \\[\n   30 - 6 = 24\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the price of the shirt after the sale is $24.\n\n2. **Calculate the price after applying the 25% off coupon to the sale price:**\n   The sale price is now $24. A 25% discount on $24 means the price is reduced by 25% of $24.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 0.25 \\times 24 = 6\n   \\]\n   So, the price after applying the coupon is:\n   \\[\n   24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the final price of the shirt after both the sale and the coupon are $18.\n\n3. **Determine the overall discount rate:**\n   The original price was $30, and the final price is $18. To find the overall discount rate, we calculate the reduction in price and then express it as a percentage of the original price.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 30 - 18 = 12\n   \\]\n   The discount rate is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount rate} = \\left( \\frac{12}{30} \\right) \\times 100 = 40\\%\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
