      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The first discount of $20\\%$ off the regular price of $\\$30$ reduces the price to:\n\\begin{align*}\n30 - 0.2 \\cdot 30 &= 30 - 6 \\\\\n&= \\24.\n\\end{align*}The second $25\\%$ discount is applied to the sale price of $\\$24$, resulting in a final price of:\n\\begin{align*}\n24 - 0.25 \\cdot 24 &= 24 - 6 \\\\\n&= \\18.\n\\end{align*}To find the single percent discount that would yield a final price of $\\$18$, we need to determine the $x\\%$ discount applied to the original price of $\\$30$ such that the final price is $\\$18$. Setting up an equation, we have:\n\\begin{align*}\n30 - \\frac{x \\cdot 30}{100} &= 18.\n\\end{align*}Solving for $x$, we get:\n\\begin{align*}\n30 - \\frac{30x}{100} &= 18,\n\\end{align*}and\n\\begin{align*}\n30 - 1.8x &= 18.\n\\end{align*}Subtracting $30$ from both sides, we have:\n\\begin{align*}\n-1.8x &= -12.\n\\end{align*}Dividing both sides by $-1.8$, we find:\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= \\frac{-12}{-1.8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{1.8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{120}{18} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 6.67\\%.\n\\end{align*}Therefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is $\\boxed{6.67\\%}$.The answer is: 6.67",
